\subsection{Flavor and system dependence}
% Two effects may lead to a particle type dependence.  First, differences between particles (\lam) and their antiparticles  (\alam) may be expected because more \lam than \alam are believed to be produced in quark jets, but gluon jets produce equal amounts of \lam and \alam.  The \alam/\lam ratio in central \Cu collisions is 0.83 $\pm$ 0.02 \cite{antsthesis}, so it is likely that both \lam and \alam production at high-\pT are dominated by gluon jets.  
Differences between baryons and mesons have been observed in \RAA and in \vtwo and models such as Recombination hypothesizes novel methods for production of baryons and mesons in \AplusA collisions to explain the observed differences.  If particles in jets are produced by vacuum fragmentation, baryon/meson ratios would be expected to be comparable to \pp.  Jets with leading baryons may have different properties than jets with leading mesons.  Particles produced by mechanisms such as Recombination would have baryon/meson ratios comparable to the bulk.  No statistically significant differences were observed between \lam and \alam triggers or \xibary and \axibary so these trigger particles were added together to increase the statistical significance of the results for comparisons to \kaon trigger particles.  The \dphi was used because these measurements were statistically limited and the \jlc is broader for identified particles than for unidentified particles.

