\Fref{Omega3cuts} compares the \myomega correlations using cut set 1 to those using cut sets 2 and 3.  Cut set 2 provides the purest \myomega sample, which might make the signal stronger if contamination from the background in the \myomega mass peak weakens the signal.  Cut set 3 provides the least pure sample meaning there is significant contamination from background in the \myomega mass peak, however, it also provides higher statistics.  There are no significant differences between the signals using different cuts.  Since the \myomega triggered correlations were within error of both the background and the signal expected from other trigger particles, it is not possible to get a statistically significant measurement of \myomega triggered di-hadron correlations in \Cu collisions at \sNNtwohundred.

\section{Summary}
No differences are observed in the \jlc between \dAu, \Cu and \Au collisions as a function of \pttrig and \ptassoc.  A system size dependence is observed, with the \jlc increasing by about 50\% from \dAu collisions to central \Au collisions at \sNNtwohundred.  The \jlc is somewhat broader in \Au collisions at \sNNtwohundred than in \Cu collisions at \sNNtwohundred.  No trigger particle type dependence is observed.

The \ridge is observed for the first time in collisions at \sNNsixtytwo and is observed in \Cu collisions.  The \ridge is independent of the collision system at a given \npart, and the \nridge/\njet ratio is independent of collision energy for the kinematic cuts studied.  No trigger particle dependence is observed.